keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - reward hacking
  - limited liability
  - option pricing
  - counterfactual reward
  - gambling for resurrection
  - simulation-based evaluation
  - backtesting
  - reward misspecification
  - electricity markets
  - imbalance settlement
  - collateral
  - pre-registration
abstract: >-
  Reinforcement-learning (RL) traders are trained and evaluated in simulators. A
  simulator's accounting decides who bears a loss beyond the agent's capital, and
  many forgive it: they floor equity at zero, write off the overshoot, or leave the
  bankruptcy step unrewarded. This hands the agent a free option. RL agents learn to
  exercise it by gambling near ruin, while evaluation reports a profit. Five
  accounting rules form an ordered ladder of such options, and approximate closed
  forms give their value. A finer time step removes a capped loss's subsidy but
  raises a zero floor's to twice that of terminal limited liability. In 2,295
  registered agents on Nordic electricity spreads and three public trading
  simulators, the exploit was learned where training reached a forgiven floor. In
  the open-source backtester backtesting.py, PPO learned to bet maximal leverage
  near ruin in 12 of 16 registered seeds on a market without edge, against 1 of 16
  in backtrader, which books the loss. No log whose market moves stay short of a
  floor can reveal it, however long; one query on a clone past the floor can. Along
  the market input, the reward is the booked profit plus a convex option payoff, so
  its shape locates hidden floors. Rewards built from a sign-reversed clone step
  removed the exploit in every registered test and mostly kept the honest edge,
  including where ruin penalties failed because losses were floored per account. In
  an electricity-trading engine, shortcuts ranked first a controller reporting +437%
  while booking -2,980%.
